* Starting Gate

# This is the only list that decides when you start writing. When these eight
# boxes are ticked, stop planning and begin. Everything else is discovered by
# writing, and what is discovered by writing is better than what is decided in
# a table.
#
# The first six come from the Core below; the last two do not.
#
# If you have gone three weeks without writing narrative, you are not
# designing: you are designing in order not to write. Come back to this list
# and check whether you had already crossed it.

- [ ] Theme and at least five Stances
- [ ] Protagonist and opponent with Ghost, Lie, Weakness, Desire and Need
- [ ] Stakes
- [ ] Ending chosen
- [ ] Point of view decided
- [ ] Premise
- [ ] The thirteen non-negotiable plot points
- [ ] The first three scenes

* Core

# Twelve questions. Short answers. One page in total.
# Questions 4 to 7 are the causal chain and are answered in that order,
# because each comes out of the one before: the Ghost produces the Lie, the Lie
# produces the Weakness, and the Weakness generates the Desire. If one does not
# fit the one beside it, the wrong one is usually the one above.

- Theme ::
  # The moral problem. One sentence carrying a risk if you fail to act a
  # certain way: "if you do not fight injustice, you become part of it."
  # It may be a question rather than a statement if you write by discovery,
  # but it has to be a statement before revision Pass 2.

- Stances ::
  # How many ways can it be answered? At least five, worst to best.
  # The list is not copied: it is built for your theme, and it is the most
  # profitable work in the whole Core. For each one, look for who could embody
  # it best: there is half your cast. The ones left over are your subplots.
