- Protagonist and their opening Stance ::

- Ghost ::
  # What happened before the story starts and still hurts. A concrete event,
  # not a climate: not "he had a hard childhood", but the exact night he
  # cannot get out of his head.

- Lie ::
  # What he concluded from it, still believes, and is not true.

- Psychological Weakness ::
  # How he behaves because he believes it. The one that holds him back.

- Moral Weakness ::
  # Who he harms, and in what concrete act.

- Desire ::

- Need ::

- Opponent and their Stance ::
  # Who wants the same thing. Their full chain goes in their own profile, in
  # objects/characters.org. The Starting Gate requires it.

- Stakes ::
  # What is lost if he fails, and how that gets worse halfway through.
  # Concrete, not abstract: not "freedom", but "his sister goes back to prison
  # and he loses custody of the girl".

- Ending ::
  # Where does he end up on the list of Stances, and does he get what he was
  # after? Two independent questions. See "The ending", below.

- Point of view ::
  # Who tells this, in what person and what tense. See its own section.

- Premise ::
  # When (problem, setting), (protagonist) must (action) in order to (resolve
  # the problem). It does not include the answer to the ending: you know the
  # ending, the premise does not say it. It also serves to pitch the story.

* World Problem

# What makes the status quo untenable: something affecting the story's world
# and everything in it. Expressed as an Aspect, so you can invoke it whenever
# you need to complicate a scene.
#
# It is not decoration: in revision, every scene where it does not show up in
# any way — not even in the background — is a candidate for cutting.
#
# Not to be confused with Trouble: Trouble belongs to a character, the World
# Problem belongs to everyone.

- Aspect ::

